ount fibre optic manometry catheter for in-vivo colonic diagnostics</article-title><source>Opt Express</source><year>2009</year><volume>17</volume><issue>25</issue><fpage>22423</fpage><lpage>22431</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">20052166</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="b32-mder-4-059"><label>32</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brandt</surname><given-names>LJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Boley</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Sammertano</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Carbon dioxide and room air insufflation of the colon</article-title><source>Gastrointest Endosc</source><year>1986</year><volume>32</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>324</fpage><lpage>329</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3095168</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="b33-mder-4-059"><label>33</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hagger</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Kumar</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Grundy</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Periodic colonic motor activity identified by 24-h pancolonic ambulatory manometry in humans</article-title><source>Neurogastroenterol Motil</source><year>2002</year><volume>14</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>271</fpage><lpage>278</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12061912</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="b34-mder-4-059"><label>34</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kozarek</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Sanowski</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Use of pressure release valve to prevent colonic injury during colonoscopy</article-title><source>Gastrointest Endosc</source><year>1980</year><volume>26</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>139</fpage><lpage>142</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7450461</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="f1-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>The top view of the Active Colonoscopy Training Model.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b15-mder-4-059">15</xref> The base is the conventional colonoscopy training model with an artificial ex vivo colon, and 10 low profile load cells, 24 light sensors, and pressure transducers are integrated with the drivers and the data acquisition system.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f1"/></fig><fig id="f2-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>The low profile load cell (4 wire full bridge) with the driver (left) and the load cells installed in the Colonoscopy Training Model (right). The rubber ring is mounted on each load cell and the artificial colon will be installed through the rubber rings to measure the forces from the colonoscope.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Twenty-four cadmium sulfide (CdS) photoconductive photocell drivers, which are adjustable for the different intensity of the light source and environment. Only digital values (on/off) are transmitted to the data acquisition system for the localization.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f3"/></fig><fig id="f4-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Intensity of light source with varying distance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b15-mder-4-059">15</xref></p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f4"/></fig><fig id="f5-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Calibration of the pressure sensor.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f5"/></fig><fig id="f6-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Interface software using LabView.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f6"/></fig><fig id="f7-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Force vs time from 10 load cells in the colonoscope training model.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f7"/></fig><fig id="f8-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Force measurement of each sensor with respect to the operation time.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f8"/></fig><fig id="f9-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Responses of the light sensors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b15-mder-4-059">15</xref></p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f9"/></fig><fig id="f10-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Air insertion to the Active Colonoscopy Training Model through the colonoscope air channel.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f10"/></fig><fig id="f11-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Figure 11</label><caption><p>Shape changes of the artificial colon in the Active Colonoscopy Training Model with respect to the pressure increment.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mder-4-059f11"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-mder-4-059" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Light Intensity (lux) with the Active Colonoscopy Training Model cover<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b15-mder-4-059">15</xref></p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No.</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Position 1</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Position 2</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Position 3</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Position 4</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7.6</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6.9</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6.2</td><td align="left" valign